markdown <<MARKDOWN
# Seeing the Filters

Filters shape what shows up in a feed, but they are not always easy to notice. In the last level, you compared two feeds and saw how filters can emphasize some ideas while leaving others out.

In this level, you’ll review a few key terms and think about how platforms can make filtering more transparent.

### **Terms to Know**

<iframe src="https://amandadennard-dotcom.github.io/Unit4_testing/vocab_flip_cards.html" width="100%" height="280" style="border:none;" title="Vocabulary flip cards"></iframe>

---

## Transparency Check: What would you want to see?

Many platforms apply filters quietly. But designers can make filters more transparent.
Below are two interface styles. One makes filtering visible. One does not.

👉 Which design feels more trustworthy? Why?

<div style="display:flex; gap:16px; flex-wrap:wrap; justify-content:center; margin-top:10px;"> <div style="background:#ffffff; border:1px solid #ddd; border-radius:12px; padding:14px; width:340px;"> <div style="font-size:12pt; font-weight:bold; margin-bottom:8px;">Version 1: Transparent</div> <div style="background:#dccbea; border:1px solid #8c52ba; border-radius:10px; padding:10px; font-size:12pt; margin-bottom:10px;"> <strong>Active filters:</strong> Following • Education • Last 7 days<br> <span style="font-size:11pt;">(You can edit these)</span> </div> <div style="font-size:12pt; color:#444;">Users can tell what’s shaping the feed and adjust it.</div> </div> <div style="background:#ffffff; border:1px solid #ddd; border-radius:12px; padding:14px; width:340px;"> <div style="font-size:12pt; font-weight:bold; margin-bottom:8px;">Version 2: Hidden</div> <div style="background:#dccbea; border:1px solid #8c52ba; border-radius:10px; padding:10px; font-size:12pt; margin-bottom:10px;"> <strong>For you</strong><br> <span style="font-size:11pt;">(No explanation)</span> </div> <div style="font-size:12pt; color:#444;">Users may assume this is “what’s out there,” not what was selected.</div> </div> </div>

<br>

<details style="background-color: #ececec; padding: 10px; margin-bottom: 10px; border: 1px solid transparent; border-radius: 5px;">
    <summary style="font-size: 1.5em; font-weight: bold;">💡 Check your thinking:</summary>
<div style="margin-top: 20px; margin-left: 20px; margin-right: 20px; margin-bottom:75px;">


  <div style="margin-top:14px;">
    <p style="font-size:13pt;line-height:1.45;margin:0;">
      Version 1 may feel more trustworthy because it shows which filters are shaping the feed and lets the user edit them. Version 2 may look simple, but it hides why certain content was selected. When filters are hidden, people may think they are seeing a complete or neutral view, even though the algorithm is still choosing what appears.
    </p>
  </div>
</details>


MARKDOWN
